{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :landing-gap-recurrence :event/note "THIRD consecutive wave where a prior session authored implementations and landed none. A prior wave-12 session ran 21:49-21:59 on 2026-08-13 against the SAME 20 candidates: 12 renderers (24-49 KB each) left UNCOMMITTED in _wt12-<short> worktrees, 1 committed-but-never-pushed (isic-0144 06cba18), 1 pushed-but-never-merged (isic-2822 960cd23). Waves 7 and 11 recorded the identical shape. Authoring has never been the bottleneck; landing is." :event/why-invisible "The prior session left no ledger row, so from the tick's perspective the pool simply had not moved. A worktree full of uncommitted work and a worktree that was never created look identical to every automated check we run."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :dead-actor :event/severity :high :event/repo "cloud-itonami-isic-889" :event/note "The actor had NEVER executed. socialworkops.operation called (g/compile ...) but langgraph.graph exports compile-graph, so the namespace failed to load entirely; no test requires that namespace, and clojure -M:run was broken too. The repo presented as complete: full .cljc module set, 41 passing tests, docs. Compounding it, socialworkops.sim invoked the CompiledGraph as (g {...}) -- it is IRunnable, not IFn -- caught every ClassCastException, and printed 'All scenarios completed without error.' Success reported while nothing ran. Both fixed in this wave; building the renderer is what first executed the graph." :event/lesson "A green test suite that does not require the namespace under test is not evidence the namespace loads. This is the ADR-2608136000 class (silence accumulating as green) at repo scale rather than gate scale."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :approver-attribution :event/note "The 'five store behaviours' recorded in wave 11 is superseded. Measuring all 20 independently produced at least EIGHT distinct shapes: (1) no approval path exists at all -- store has no write fn (isic-0144, isic-920); (2) drops entirely (isic-1074, isic-8219); (3) keeps cleanly (isic-8129-facade, isic-889); (4) kept on :payload but absent from :value (isic-7912, isic-5630); (5) kept by register-writing effects, dropped by effects that re-derive the record from the registry (isic-4659, isic-2822, isic-9103, isic-8521, isic-6492, isic-6621, isic-9000, isic-8411-electoral); (6) paperwork keeps it, money-movement drops it (isic-6530); (7) commit path keeps it, REJECT path drops it (isic-931); (8) store drops it but the audit ledger keeps it (isic-872) -- the inverse of the common case (isic-862 is the reverse again: drops on approval, keeps on rejection)." :event/pattern "Where the split is by effect, the dropped side is consistently the highest-stakes op: publish-receipt (electoral), disbursement (pension), loan-approved/disbursed (credit), production-release, mark-transferred/released. commit-record! rebuilds those from the registry and never reads :payload. The approver of the consequential act is the one not recorded." :event/method "Every agent derived the disclosure at RENDER time by scanning registers for approver-shaped keys, so the pages self-correct if a store is fixed; none hard-coded 'this repo has the defect'."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :orchestrator-error :event/note "I briefed all 20 agents with a FALSE premise -- 'no render_html exists on main or any branch' -- caused by two bugs in my own pre-flight. (1) I tested for prior worktrees at _wt12-$code where $code still carried the 'isic-' prefix, so it stat'd directories that never existed and reported 0 where 12 uncommitted implementations sat. (2) A printf column used `echo -`, which zsh swallows, blanking an entire column rather than erroring. Both are the shape the repo's own guidance names: a check that could not measure returned the same value as a check that measured and found nothing. Four agents independently corrected me; the salvage outcome was good, but that was the agents' doing, not the briefing's." :event/lesson "Pre-flight measurement for a wave needs the same evidence floor demanded of gates: assert the probe found SOMETHING, and print what was scanned, not just what matched."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :fake-console-verdicts :event/note "Two repos carried a console on main with no generator. Verdicts differ from wave 7's outright fabrications, so 'hand-written' must not be equated with 'fabricated'. isic-4659 (7,787 B): hand-authored and never generated (landed in the initial commit, contains 18 disabled <button> elements, zero execution artefacts -- no dispatch/invoice numbers, no ledger, no confidence values) BUT every one of its 9 order ids, order numbers, counterparties and per-order governor verdicts matches the seed exactly. Accurate, unverifiable, now superseded. isic-2822 (4,603 B): same hand-authored provenance, invents no entity, but unit-1's status 'ready' is FABRICATED -- grep finds 'ready' in src/ and test/ only inside the word 'already'; the sole :status the codebase assigns is :intake, which the real page now renders. Also stale (predates :issue-maintenance-notice)." :event/detector "Only running the generator and diffing discriminates. Domain-name checks do not: both files used correct domain vocabulary."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :hold-conflation :event/note "The governor-refusal vs phase/rollout-gate distinction was live in nearly every repo, and every agent proved its guard discriminates rather than asserting it -- typically by cutting the HARD-hold scenarios while KEEPING the phase-gate holds and confirming -main threw and wrote no file. Concrete counts: isic-8219's ledger contains :t :governor-hold ten times but only nine are refusals (s02 is the byte-identical request to s04 run at phase 1, :violations []); isic-8521 would report 8 instead of 6; isic-889 7 instead of 4; isic-6492's :approval-rejected fact CARRIES a violation ([{:rule :approver-rejected}]) so classifying on :violations alone miscounts it as a governor refusal -- it must classify on :t first. isic-6621 found a page calling a governor ESCALATION a violation because :ok? is (= 0 code) meaning commit-eligible, not clean." :event/also "isic-8129-facade found the salvaged renderer's invariant asserted on an :approval-granted LEDGER fact that the commit node never appends -- the guard could not pass no matter how many humans approved. A guard that cannot be satisfied is indistinguishable from a build that legitimately fails."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :verification-method :event/note "Techniques that discriminated, worth reusing: (1) isic-8411-electoral excluded :actor from its approver scan -- ledger facts carry :actor (the EXECUTING actor-id) and in that demo it equals the approver string, so reading it would have shown a confident false 'retained'. A wrong answer that agrees with the right one on your test data is the hardest kind to catch. (2) isic-931 kept a per-run UUID and wall-clock ms OUT of the rendered page and then verified neither appears, rather than discovering non-determinism after the fact. (3) isic-2822 hit the [op subject-id] join trap for real: unique for dispatch/certificate (double-issuance guards) but NOT for maintenance notices (no guard); verified counterfactually against a real run and extended the scenario so the second notice is visible. (4) isic-920 and isic-862 disclosed OBSERVED GAPS on the page rather than fixing the governor inside a rendering task -- isic-920's self-exclusion enrolment phrasing escapes HARD CHECK 3's keyword list and commits. (5) isic-872 measured that an allowlisted op can NEVER pass because the advisor's own rationale contains a substring the scope filter matches -- a real over-block, shown as declared-gate vs observed-disposition side by side." :event/cross-repo "isic-6621 surfaced a conflict of interest on party-5 visible ONLY via cross-reference to cloud-itonami-isic-8291 corporate intelligence; a local-only screen clears that party. First wave where one industry actor's refusal depends on another's data."}
{:event/type :industry-stack/finding :event/wave 12 :event/at "2026-08-14" :event/kind :not-done :event/note "(a) ~30 stray worktrees remain under orgs/cloud-itonami/ (_wt12-*, _wt12b-*, _wt12w-*, plus wave5-7 residue). TWELVE of the _wt12-* still hold the prior session's uncommitted renderers -- now redundant with what landed, but do NOT git worktree remove them blind; they were the only copy until this wave. (b) isic-2822 has a competing prior branch origin/agent/flagship-item2-2822 (960cd23) left unmerged and undeleted; superseded by what landed. (c) Stale agent/flagship-item2-<short> branches untouched in all 20. (d) NO page was opened in a browser in any repo -- all 20 verified structurally (tag balance, class vocabulary against jp-go-dds.skin, no nil/%s leaks) but visual DADS rendering is unconfirmed fleet-wide. (e) jp-go-dds resolves from the local ~/.gitlibs cache at pinned sha 83db9954; cold-cache/no-egress resolution untested, which matters because fleet nodes have non-uniform egress. (f) DatomicStore path unexercised in isic-8411-electoral, isic-8521, isic-862 -- approver findings there are MemStore-only. (g) No fleet-ci gate was run against any of these branches." :event/repo-defects-left-open "isic-920 self-exclusion keyword gap; isic-872 coordinate-supply-request permanent over-block; isic-889 operation/build docstring promises interrupt-before but hard-codes compile opts without it; isic-862 sim's 'effect not :propose' scenario does not hold because advisor hardcodes :effect :propose."}
